he robot's planned path on the map and its real-world environment, illustrating how the robot navigates and operates within the space.)/K 2/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj14782 0 obj<</K 3/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj14783 0 obj<</K 4/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14784 0 obj<</K[5 14938 0 R 7]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14785 0 obj<</K[8 14932 0 R 10 14933 0 R 13 14934 0 R 16 14935 0 R 19 14936 0 R 22]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14786 0 obj<</ActualText(\\bALT{} \\label{eq:lissajous} t \\mapsto\\binom{A_x \\sin \\left\(K\\omega_x t\\right\)}{A_y \\sin \\left\(K\\omega_y t\\right\)}\\eALT{} )/Border[0 0 0]/K 24/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14787 0 obj<</K 27/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14788 0 obj<</K 29/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14789 0 obj<</K[30 14930 0 R 32]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14790 0 obj<</K 33/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1379 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14791 0 obj<</K 2/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1383 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14792 0 obj<</K 4/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1383 0 R/S/H2/Type/StructElem>>endobj14793 0 obj<</K 5/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1383 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14794 0 obj<</A 14929 0 R/Alt(Two side-by-side box plots compare errors in rotational and translational measurements at different speed factors labeled 0.013, 0.026, 0.052, and 0.104. The left plot shows rotational error in degrees, with smaller errors and less variation at lower spee\d factors, increasing notably at the highest speed factor. The right plot shows translational error in centimeters, which also grows larger and more variable as speed factor increases. Each speed factor has one or two groups indicated by numbers in parent\heses below the labels, showing that error tends to rise with speed and varies between groups. This comparison highlights how measurement accuracy decreases as speed increases for both rotation and translation.)/K 6/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1383 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj14795 0 obj<</K 7/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1383 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj14796 0 obj<</K[9 14923 0 R 11 14924 0 R 13 14925 0 R 16 14926 0 R 19]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1383 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14797 0 obj<</K 1/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1386 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14798 0 obj<</A 14922 0 R/Alt(Two side-by-side box plots compare errors in rotational and translational measurements at different acceleration levels, ranging from 0.05 to 0.3 meters per second squared. The left plot shows rotational error in degrees, with median values generally betw\een 0 and 0.5 degrees, and variability increasing at higher accelerations. The right plot shows translational error in centimeters, with median errors mostly between 2 and 8 centimeters, also varying with acceleration. This comparison highlights how measu\rement errors change with acceleration for rotational and translational movements, helping to understand accuracy under different conditions.)/K 2/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1386 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj14799 0 obj<</K 3/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1386 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj14800 0 obj<</K[5 14918 0 R 7 14919 0 R 10 14920 0 R 13]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1386 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14801 0 obj<</K[14 14908 0 R 16 14909 0 R 18 14910 0 R 20 14911 0 R 22 14912 0 R 24]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1386 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14802 0 obj<</K 1/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1389 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14803 0 obj<</A 14907 0 R/Alt(Four line graphs compare trajectory error and velocity over time. On the left, three graphs show trajectory error in x, y, and z directions, each fluctuating between about -0.5 and 1.5 centimeters over 20 seconds. On the right, two graphs show velocity: t\he top graph has a repeating triangular wave pattern between 0 and 1 meter per second, while the bottom graph shows velocity magnitude with rapid fluctuations mostly below 0.5 meters per second. This comparison highlights how position errors vary in three\ dimensions alongside changes in velocity during the same time period.)/K 2/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1389 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj14804 0 obj<</K[3 14906 0 R 6]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1389 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj14805 0 obj<</A 14905 0 R/Alt(Two box plots compare error measurements at different velocities from 0.25 to 1.0 meters per second. The left plot shows rotational error in radians, with errors mostly near zero except at 1.0 meters per second where the median error is negative. The righ\t plot shows error in centimeters in the direction of travel, with median errors increasing up to 0.75 meters per second and then decreasing at 1.0 meters per second. This comparison highlights how error varies with speed in two different movement aspects\, helping to understand accuracy changes in rotational and travel directions.)/K 8/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1389 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj14806 0 obj<</K 9/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1389 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj14807 0 obj<</K[11 14899 0 R 13 14900 0 R 15 14901 0 R 17]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1389 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14808 0 obj<</A 14898 0 R/Alt(Two side-by-side box plots compare errors in rotational and translational measurements at different acceleration levels, shown on the horizontal axis in radians per second squared. The left plot shows rotational error in degrees, with error values mostly \between negative two and four degrees, varying across acceleration levels. The right plot shows translational error in centimeters, with a wider range of errors from zero to about nine centimeters, also varying with acceleration. The plots highlight how e\rror changes with acceleration for rotational and translational movements, showing that translational errors tend to be larger and more variable than rotational errors. This comparison helps understand the accuracy limits of measurements under different a\cceleration conditions.)/K 2/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1392 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj14809 0 obj<</K 3/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1392 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj14810 0 obj<</A 14897 0 R/Alt(Five line graphs show changes over time in different movement-related measurements. On the left, three graphs display position errors in x direction, y direction, and angle theta, each fluctuating around zero with varying patterns. On the right, two graph\s show velocity measures: linear velocity with small rapid changes, and angular velocity with a repeating triangular wave pattern. The graphs illustrate how position errors and velocities vary during a 26-second period, highlighting differences in movemen\t accuracy and speed. This helps understand the relationship between errors in position and the velocities involved.)/K 5/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1392 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj14811 0 obj<</K[6 14896 0 R 9]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1392 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj14812 0 obj<</A 14895 0 R/Alt(Two side-by-side box plots compare error measurements at different velocities for rotational and translational movements. The left plot shows rotational error in radians per second at four velocity levels, with errors ranging roughly from negative one to \one point two. The right plot shows translational error in centimeters at the same velocity levels, with errors increasing from near zero to about seven centimeters. Both plots indicate that error tends to increase as velocity increases, highlighting how \higher speeds lead to greater measurement inaccuracies in both rotational and translational movements.)/K 11/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1392 0 R/S/Figure/Type/StructElem>>endobj14813 0 obj<</K 12/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1392 0 R/S/Caption/Type/StructElem>>endobj14814 0 obj<</K 1/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1395 0 R/S/P/Type/StructElem>>endobj14815 0 obj<</K 3/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1395 0 R/S/H2/Type/StructElem>>endobj14816 0 obj<</K[4 14894 0 R 7]/P 14748 0 R/Pg 1395 0 R/S